Amazon Linux 2023 / RHEL / Fedora:

wget -q -O gpg.key https://rpm.grafana.com/gpg.key
sudo rpm --import gpg.key
echo -e '[grafana]\nname=grafana\nbaseurl=https://rpm.grafana.com\nrepo_gpgcheck=1\nenabled=1\ngpgcheck=1\ngpgkey=https://rpm.grafana.com/gpg.key\nsslverify=1\nsslcacert=/etc/pki/tls/certs/ca-bundle.crt' | sudo tee /etc/yum.repos.d/grafana.repo


3. Install Alloy
Amazon Linux/RHEL/Fedora:

sudo dnf install alloy

4. Get your Grafana Cloud connection details (do this before configuring)

In Grafana Cloud → your stack's My Account page → find the Prometheus and Loki "Details" panels. Each gives you:

A remote-write URL
A numeric username/instance ID
An API token you generate right there (scope it for metrics:write and logs:write)

##### Prometheus
Remote Write URL:  https://prometheus-prod-37-prod-ap-southeast-1.grafana.net/api/prom/push
Username / instance ID: <PROM_INSTANCE_ID>
Password: <GRAFANA_CLOUD_PROM_TOKEN>   # glc_... token, do NOT commit the real value

##### Loki
Name: grafanacloud-rubymirror735-logs
URL: https://logs-prod-020.grafana.net
User: <LOKI_INSTANCE_ID>
Password: <GRAFANA_CLOUD_LOKI_TOKEN>   # glc_... token, do NOT commit the real value

Keep those four values handy — you'll paste them into Alloy's config next.

5. Configure Alloy

The default config file lives at /etc/alloy/config.alloy. Edit it:

sudo nano /etc/alloy/config.alloy
alloy fmt /etc/alloy/config.alloy

6. Start and enable it

sudo systemctl start alloy
systemctl restart alloy
sudo systemctl enable alloy.service   # start on boot
sudo systemctl status alloy           # verify it's running
sudo journalctl -u alloy -f           # tail logs / debug config errors
journalctl -e -u alloy

7. Verify in Grafana Cloud

In your Grafana Cloud instance → Explore → select the Prometheus data source → run:

up

